# Portable, agnostic harness

This harness/profile is **shareable**. Every skill, rule, learning, fix,
and plan written at profile level must work for someone who clones the
repo — not only for one developer's laptop layout.

## Skills

1. **Project-agnostic by default.** Teach procedures (dataset design, fine-tuning
   recipes, eval rubrics, literature review) without assuming a
   specific repo name, hostname, or hardware nickname.
2. **Examples use placeholders**, not private codenames:
   - Good: "your project at `$PROJECT_ROOT`"
   - Bad: "project at `/home/alice/my-private-app` on `edge-box.internal`"
3. **Hardware class is OK; nicknames are not.** "8 GB unified memory class
   device" is portable. A named host at a private IP is not.
4. **Split portable vs project-local knowledge:**
   | Portable (skill / profile rule) | Project-local (repo `.hermes/` or AGENTS.md) |
   |----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
   | How to design an eval rubric     | This project's rubric JSON + frozen test SHA  |
   | VRAM estimation for a recipe     | This run's exact checkpoint path              |
   | Remote API quirks (generic)      | Which provider this project uses today        |
5. **When saving a new skill**, ask: "Would this make sense if every path
   and project name were replaced with `$PROJECT_ROOT`?" If not, generalize
   or move the project-specific part to the project's `.hermes/learnings/`.

## Rules, learnings, fixes

- Same bar as skills: no usernames, home directories, or one-off cron IDs.
- Learnings capture **patterns** ("this recipe fits a 7B model in ~5 GB on
  Orin-class hardware"), not session state.
- Fixes capture **failure modes** ("don't paste session keys into repo
  files"), not one user's incident timestamps.

## Naming

- Prefer generic skill names: `qlora-pipeline`, `eval-rubric-design`
  over private project codenames.
- If a skill is tied to one external tool, name the **tool**
  (`trackio`, `arxiv`), not a private deployment.

## Review checklist (before adding to the harness)

- [ ] No `/home/<user>/` paths
- [ ] No real hostnames, IPs, or cron UUIDs
- [ ] No API keys or token-shaped strings (except documented placeholders)
- [ ] Env vars documented; values never inlined
- [ ] Readable by someone who only has the harness + this profile — no oral history
